{"id": "d987c655-9fdd-474f-a4bd-9b9d107c5f39", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T13:18:30.470884", "source": "user", "parent_id": "08bc3060-40c7-407f-b6a4-e6ad9a5d0ace", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"We need a design-only experiment that compares JaxMARL's PPO implementation to a Q-Learning approach in SMAX environments, evaluated on training time and normalized score. This is a design brief, not an execution task; no code runs are required and repositories/compute are out of scope, but you may consult any locally available references if useful. Right now, please do only the following and stop: (1) Briefly inspect the workspace and any locally available references relevant to JaxMARL, SMAX, PPO, and MARL Q-Learning; report what is present and any constraints you observe. (2) Sketch a proposed experimental approach at a high level: candidate SMAX maps/scenarios to include, the two algorithms to compare (PPO via JaxMARL vs. a Q-Learning variant — propose which one, e.g., VDN/QMIX/IQL, with rationale) plus shared vs. differing components, training-time and normalized-score definitions and how each will be measured, controls (seeds, hardware normalization, environment wrappers, network capacity, episode budgets), and the analysis/comparison plan (statistical tests, reporting format, ablations). (3) List the consequential assumptions and uncertainties you are making (e.g., expected compute budget, number of seeds, exact Q-Learning algorithm choice, SMAX map selection, reward shaping, evaluation protocol, whether to share encoders, normalization of training time across libraries). Do not produce the final design document yet. Return your findings as a concise report with those three sections.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How does JaxMARL’s PPO compare to Q-Learning in SMAX environments in terms of training time and normalized score?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [\"neurips2024/97649/0:setup_2\", \"neurips2024/97649/0:setup_4\", \"neurips2024/97649/0:setup_12\"], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
